<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT HAPPENS TO A MACROSTEP THAT CANNOT END?

  W3C SCXML 3.13's Principles and Constraints say a macrostep *may not
  terminate*, and that this "is currently allowed". A document with a cyclic
  eventless transition is therefore not malformed — it is a document whose
  macrostep is infinite, and an engine that ran it to the letter would never
  return.

  SCE does not run it to the letter. It stops at a microstep ceiling and reports
  through `truncated_macrosteps` + `last_truncated_macrostep_state`, because
  **every other reading a host takes says the opposite**: `get_current_state`
  answers, `is_running` is true, and the call returned in microseconds. The
  configuration behind those answers is not the stable one 3.13 promises.

  ⚠⚠⚠⚠ REGISTER ITEM 551, AND THIS IS THE PICTURE WITH NO ERRORS IN IT.
  `document::faults` already reads the cascade — *"the document DOES match the
  error, the handler fails the same way, and the drain never empties … a core at
  full tilt with a configuration that never moves"*. That sentence is true of
  this document too, and NOT ONE `error.*` IS RAISED HERE. So the two counters
  `faults` used to read are both zero on a machine that is exactly the failure
  they were written to catch.

  ⚠⚠ THE DATAMODEL IS `null` ON PURPOSE, and it is what makes the control
  structural rather than observed. With no `<data>`, no `cond`, no `<send>` and
  no `<invoke>`, this document has nothing that COULD raise an error — so a gate
  reading `unanswered == 0 && cascaded == 0` here is reading a fact about the
  document rather than about how the run happened to go. `probe_unanswered.scxml`
  is the opposite corner of the same axis: an error and no truncation, against a
  truncation and no error.

  ⚠ ASKED BY COMPILING AND RUNNING, this crate's probe convention: whether a
  NULL-transition cycle truncates on THIS engine at THIS pin is a fact about the
  engine, and reading the specification is not measuring it.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="probe_truncated"
       datamodel="null"
       initial="spinning">

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠ THE SUBJECT: two eventless transitions pointing at each other. Neither
    carries an event, so both are enabled by NULL, and the drain that runs them
    has work again the moment it finishes — which is the definition of a
    macrostep that cannot reach a stable configuration.

    ⚠ TWO STATES RATHER THAN ONE SELF-TRANSITION, deliberately: a self-transition
    is a shape an engine may special-case (SCE's own suite records one swallowing
    a `<parallel>` root — see `probe_parallel.scxml`), and a cycle through two
    distinct states cannot be mistaken for that. It also makes
    `last_truncated_macrostep_state` a reading with something to say: the state
    named is one the cycle actually passes through.
  -->
  <state id="spinning">
    <transition target="turning"/>
  </state>

  <state id="turning">
    <transition target="spinning"/>
  </state>

</scxml>
